Another important factor often considered within CP is ensuring the geometry used is representative of the microstructure present within the real material. Commonly, statistical information about microstructure can be extracted through the use of large 2D EBSD scans and then used to synthetically generate a representative volume element (RVE) [ 10 ,  16 ,  17 ,  18 – 19 ]. Whilst RVEs from 2D EBSD data can exhibit realistic behaviour, this will not hold true if the grain shape is anisotropic because the 2D representation is unable to capture the full grain structure. Lim et al. [ 7 ] worked around the 2D geometry problem by manufacturing a material with large grains (up to 1 mm). This allowed for the extrusion of one face to be able to acceptably represent the complete microstructure of the sample being analysed. Work by Zhang [ 6 ] combined a surface EBSD map and differential-aperture X-ray microscopy (DAXM) to find grain shapes in a 134  × \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
